<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hispania Citerior</h1><p class="st-meta">197 BCE – 27 BCE · capital Tarraco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rome's province of Nearer Spain, from the Ebro and the east coast into the Meseta, the base for Rome's long wars against the Celtiberians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hispania Citerior was created in 197 BCE as one of the two Roman provinces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, covering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>. It grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> wartime base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, established after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Scipio's</a> landing in 218 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A praetor or consul governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, leading near-constant campaigns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato</a> crushed the north-eastern revolt in 195 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-sempronius-gracchus-consul-177-bc">Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus</a> made treaties with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians</a> in 179 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines around Carthago Nova were among the richest in the Roman world. The province steadily pushed inland, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> in 133 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>It was the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Sertorius</a>' war from 80 to 72 BCE, ended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, and of Caesar's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilerda">Ilerda</a> in 49 BCE. In 27 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus</a> enlarged it as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis</a>, adding the north-west after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 197 BCE – 27 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.78%;width:3.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>197 BCE – 27 BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarraco</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cato the Elder, Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus, Pompey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Spain</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nearer Spain, Hither Spain</dd></dl></aside></div>
